/id #edb/ref 17592186496957} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc60e22-f056-5e38-acee-a1ff3bd5b359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497051} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fac3b2-a967-5d13-a1ea-4935f33399bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497051} :answer-field/id #uuid "963b2689-75ac-584f-b914-bf153ce7d339" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497287} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57093fa8-b5a6-51a4-91fe-a596706e217d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496842} {:answer/id #uuid "8314a3d1-ca2d-5375-bba8-185f826d8883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496926} {:answer/id #uuid "c70a6098-ad96-5098-b4cd-e316a6f02a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497068} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ef1fe8-f6c3-5596-a93b-23565d02f42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497154} {:answer/id #uuid "f70d67a8-7c41-5b09-8d78-191af0485ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497068} :answer-field/id #uuid "feee918a-d250-5e87-b14c-0c111d8be8f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "af33fe07-4f86-5eae-9df8-9d1a0ae2b710" :question/math-academy-id "q-335618" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=2x(1-x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-2y+3xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-2xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-2xy)=2-4x-2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-2xy)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+3xy)=3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+3xy)=-2+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4x-2y & -2x \\\\ 3y & -2+3x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4(0)-2(0) & -2(0) \\\\ 3(0) & -2+3(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"} :step/id #uuid "51e6cd51-40b0-57dc-ae60-04efd51a77f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497437}} {:db/id #edb/ref 1