 #uuid "b38e0e61-682b-5ba0-8a07-6ba2bc0dfa58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497025} {:answer/id #uuid "cc493d8a-a8ec-5aa1-ae81-ac9bd675a655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497080} {:answer/id #uuid "d661add0-e9ab-5def-b53e-a9b6046af0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497108} {:answer/id #uuid "fce63f78-7203-526e-af52-97c8f967d525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497025} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbaea06f-9399-5f51-8fd6-1c23afa989f4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f33a0982-c501-5349-ad00-235127306e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-335670" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=5x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-2y+3xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=5x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x(1-2x)-xy)=5-20x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+3xy)=3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+3xy)=-2+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}5-20x-y & -x \\\\ 3y & -2+3x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}5-20(0)-0 & -0 \\\\ 3(0) & -2+3(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"} :step/id #uuid "5932a145-7c28-5526-882d-17efb83e0fb8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0cca672-22d2-56b4-8615-61d6e24b7f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370418} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b070e5-7be6-50e8-a4a0-beb9ca54ef6f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370417} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dda70e62-0307-5f61-be89-38a5799d0f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370420}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370420} :answer-field/id #uuid "793a6dcc-2165-52e4-94f6-60749fcac9b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3512a5da-77e9-5ff0-a44a-28af35917f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370422} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e028e8-2b52-5edb-aa50-089cf299893a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6734dc20-5e4c-5ba3-a922-996f26cded96" :question/math-academy-id "q-335607" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=4x(1-0.25x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-2xy &=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 4x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)(1-0.25(2))-2(2)y & =0 \\\\ 4-4y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(2,1).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9c6eefcd-5b2a-58f2-8fa4-3bbf0349123c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497435}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7f7168-2238-5278-920a-322ccf51d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497010} :answer-field/id #uuid "2827ddbf-479b-5003-a2de-01715d6851fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497233} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7038e05b-62e6-5b9d-89f6-5884cc66e3ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496900}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496900} :answer-field/id #uuid "866fb4cc-629f-52ec-a9a5-882783d95536" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497280} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56c313eb-74f2-5951-b106-ca3f0b7a9669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496841} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9119afb-d7f2-5573-9b5d-ea702cbc65cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "54e98471-5688-5240-867b-3e9eae0b3046" :question/math-academy-id "q-335588" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=4x(1-0.25x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-3xy &=0 \\\\ -y+xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-1+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 4x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1)(1-0.25(1))-3(1)y & =0 \\\\ 3-3y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bdfcda17-c79f-53ce-af4f-1ad4c7ab8581" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497459}}] :activity/title "The Lotka-Volterra Model With Carrying Capacity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496650}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521585} :activity/id #uuid "e3d9b7ec-1851-512a-a201-72d69471f1e2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049289 :topic/id #uuid "ea2533e0-c4a4-5c4e-b20a-9fb6ea3d7c70" :topic/math-academy-id 3231} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a76d935c-2199-5460-a09a-2ef79825d59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521141} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb8730e-04ca-5e0f-9b3a-6b1eb07a3311" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a14350e-8f1d-5034-8c34-0c1be5812523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520931} :answer-field/id #uuid "3420ffc8-c03a-5ee6-b400-0d64013fb521" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521354} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93049ccf-2b3b-5ef9-a326-d4f971a93e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521097} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e096f83-a268-52b6-a0de-9b2e3f7fae60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d80de28b-ca2c-5fd8-ab88-370a0ca5902e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335606" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=3x(1-0.5x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-4y+4xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2xy &=0 \\\\ -4y+4xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+4xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+4x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.5x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)(1-0.5(1))-2(1)y & =0 \\\\ 1.5-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(1,0.75).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c4408e5-7391-59ee-b9e2-8bb13b7911cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5945fd09-32e0-5f46-bbe0-b89a233c1a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520969} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df6522f-2c6e-54e2-92a8-830be048fc64" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ca83931-fb18-5c7d-b857-e2bfb9743388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520939} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd1d7e76-9e39-5989-b9b2-3fd1ad45aa23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521422} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd513e1b-1d09-5226-9b47-696ee3a40809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521261} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4886f21-7c67-5733-a044-771bcc5c4cc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3f73f08-cefc-5b56-bacc-8fec801a6a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335612" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=2x(1-0.05x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3xy &=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.05x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(20,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)(1-0.05(2))-3(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-6y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,0.6).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(20,0),\\,(2,0.6).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2efaa86b-14b5-50a4-8011-f389ec1cb877" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c32c689-fe39-563b-8fe4-458134d4052b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370425} {:answer/id #uuid "d285e5c4-dc65-5536-9f79-3f681091c6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370426} {:answer/id #uuid "1c972551-9177-53db-ba32-aadf4301d9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370427} {:answer/id #uuid "a46f4309-ae1e-5db4-a422-01ee70908e05" :ans